================================================================================
Bundled TextMate grammars (syntax highlighting)
================================================================================

The grammar files under src/main/resources/com/editora/grammars/
(*.tmLanguage.json) provide syntax highlighting and come from two sources.

Most grammars were obtained from the shiki "tm-grammars" collection:

    https://github.com/shikijs/textmate-grammars-themes
    (packages/tm-grammars/grammars)

The C#, CSS, dotenv, PowerShell, SQL, Scala, and Clojure grammars are from
tm-grammars 1.32.22; the other grammars from this source are from earlier
releases. The Scala grammar (scala.tmLanguage.json) is shiki's copy of
scala/vscode-scala-syntax and the Clojure grammar (clojure.tmLanguage.json) of
the one Microsoft Visual Studio Code ships (from atom/language-clojure), both
under the MIT License; both are vendored unmodified.

These are aggregated by shiki from their respective upstream projects, each
under its original license (predominantly MIT, with some under other permissive
licenses such as Apache-2.0 or BSD); see the upstream repository above for the
per-grammar license and source attribution. This source includes the Lua,
Dockerfile, Terraform (HCL), TOML, systemd, desktop-entry, and dotenv grammars
(lua/dockerfile/terraform/toml/systemd/desktop/dotenv .tmLanguage.json; systemd,
desktop, and dotenv are under the MIT License). It also includes the SSH config
grammar (ssh-config.tmLanguage.json), which shiki aggregates from
textmate/ssh-config.tmbundle, distributed under that project's permissive
license ("Permission to copy, use, modify, sell and distribute this software is
granted ... provided 'as is' without warranty"), and the plain-text developer
format grammars (all MIT): diff (diff.tmLanguage.json) and Makefile
(makefile.tmLanguage.json), both aggregated from Microsoft Visual Studio Code
(© Microsoft Corporation); Protocol Buffers (proto.tmLanguage.json) from
zxh0/vscode-proto3; GraphQL (graphql.tmLanguage.json) from
graphql/vscode-graphql (© GraphQL Contributors); and just
(just.tmLanguage.json) from nefrob/vscode-just.

The following grammars were instead obtained from the Eclipse tm4e
"language_pack" (which curates grammars for the tm4e engine used here):

    https://github.com/eclipse-tm4e/tm4e
    (org.eclipse.tm4e.language_pack/syntaxes)

    XML, shell, C, C++, Rust, Ruby, Markdown.

The XML grammar (xml.tmLanguage.json) is extended locally: rules for the DTD
<!ELEMENT>, <!ATTLIST> and <!NOTATION> declarations (in a DOCTYPE's internal
subset and in a bare .dtd file) beside the upstream <!ENTITY> rule.

The TypeScript and TypeScriptReact grammars (typescript.tmLanguage.json,
typescriptreact.tmLanguage.json — also used for JavaScript/JSX) were obtained
from Microsoft Visual Studio Code under the MIT License:

    https://github.com/microsoft/vscode
    (extensions/typescript-basics/syntaxes)

    License: MIT  (© Microsoft Corporation)

The PHP grammar (php.tmLanguage.json) was obtained from Microsoft Visual Studio
Code under the MIT License (converted there from KapitanOczywisty/language-php):

    https://github.com/microsoft/vscode
    (extensions/php/syntaxes/php.tmLanguage.json)

    License: MIT  (© Microsoft Corporation; © GitHub Inc. / language-php)

The Astro grammar (astro.tmLanguage.json) was obtained from Astro's official
language-tools package under the MIT License:

    https://github.com/withastro/astro
    (packages/language-tools/vscode/syntaxes/astro.tmLanguage.json)

    License: MIT  (© Astro contributors)

The Caddyfile grammar (caddyfile.tmLanguage.json) was obtained from the official
Caddy VS Code extension under the MIT License:

    https://github.com/caddyserver/vscode-caddyfile
    (syntaxes/caddyfile.tmLanguage.json)

    License: MIT  (© 2023 Matthew Penner)

The crontab and Git config grammars (crontab.tmLanguage.json,
git-config.tmLanguage.json) were obtained from the TextMate bundles
textmate/cron.tmbundle and textmate/git.tmbundle (converted here from the
original plist .tmLanguage to JSON, otherwise unmodified). These bundles are
distributed under TextMate's permissive license: "Permission to copy, use,
modify, sell and distribute this software is granted ... provided 'as is'
without warranty."

    https://github.com/textmate/cron.tmbundle
    https://github.com/textmate/git.tmbundle

The fstab, hosts, and Debian/Ubuntu grammars (fstab.tmLanguage.json,
hosts.tmLanguage.json, deb822.tmLanguage.json, apt-sources.tmLanguage.json,
interfaces.tmLanguage.json, debian-changelog.tmLanguage.json) — like the HTTP
grammar (http.tmLanguage.json), the Java properties grammar
(properties.tmLanguage.json), and the Git attributes grammar
(gitattributes.tmLanguage.json), the pip requirements grammar
(pip-requirements.tmLanguage.json), the CSV, Mermaid, Typst, and log-file
grammars (csv/mermaid/typst/log .tmLanguage.json), and the HTML derivative that
the Markdown grammar embeds for raw HTML (html-derivative.tmLanguage.json,
modelled on VS Code's) — were written for Editora and are covered by Editora's
own MIT License (above).

These remain under their original upstream licenses (predominantly MIT). Apart
from the two plist-to-JSON conversions noted above, the vendored grammars are
redistributed unmodified except for these local changes (each file's
information_for_contributors header or Git history has the detail):

    applyEndPatternLast "1" written as true, the only form tm4e reads as true:
        cpp, graphql, groovy, markdown, shell, sql
    look-behinds the Joni regex backend rejects (variable length) rewritten as
    fixed-length alternatives or line-anchored begin/end rules:
        css (three selector look-behinds), desktop (Categories/Exec/Version
        values and the key before "="), markdown (strikethrough), ruby,
        typescript, typescriptreact
    markdown: the {,3} quantifier written {0,3}; front matter as one begin/end
        rule with a begin/while body; fences for Kotlin, TOML, Mermaid and
        console/shell-session, and VS Code's `shellscript` fence id
    python: extended for newer Python (PEP 695 type parameters on def/class
        and the `type` alias statement, PEP 750 t-string prefixes, an
        strftime-style f-string format spec, match/case as keywords only on a
        statement ending in a colon, and def/class/decorator names that shadow
        a builtin or are dunders scoped as declarations)
    shell: rules upstream includes but never defines; <<\EOF delimiters; more
        ${...} expansion operators; =~ right sides as regexes; &> and &>> as
        redirections; brace expansion; select/coproc keywords; zsh { cmd }

Languages bundled: Java, XML, shell, PowerShell, DOS batch, Python, Groovy,
Kotlin, Ruby, C, C++, Rust, Go, C#, Markdown, JSON, CSS, HTML, YAML, INI, SQL,
systemd, desktop entries, dotenv, SSH config, Git config, crontab, Caddyfile,
fstab, hosts, .properties, deb822 (Debian control / APT sources), APT
sources.list, network interfaces, Debian changelog, unified diff/patch,
Makefile, justfile, Protocol Buffers, GraphQL, Astro, .gitattributes, pip
requirements, Scala, Clojure.

